== Six Propositions Concerning Satan

And now, to forestall objections, I will lay down some #emph[propositions,] or #emph[conclusions,] concerning Satan and his #emph[devices,] and then give you the reasons of the point, and so come to make some use and application of the whole to ourselves.

#strong[The first #emph[proposition] or #emph[conclusion] I will lay down is this.]

That though Satan has his #emph[devices] to draw souls to sin, yet we must be careful that we do not lay all our temptations on Satan, that we do not wrong the devil and father on him what is to be fathered on our own base hearts. I think that men often charge on the devil what is to be charged on their own hearts. “Then the #smallcaps[Lord] God said to the#footnote[Gen 3:13.] woman, ‘What is this you have done?’ ‘The serpent deceived me,’ she replied, ‘and I ate.’” Sin and shifting the blame came into the world together. It is no small baseness of our hearts that they will be wicked, yes, very wicked, and yet will father that wickedness on Satan. Man has an evil root within him, so that, were there no devil to tempt him, nor any wicked men in the world to entice him, yet that root of bitterness, that cursed sinful nature that is in him, would draw him to sin, though he knows beforehand that the wages of sin is eternal death. “For out of the heart come evil thoughts, murder, adultery,#footnote[Rom 6:23. Matt 15:19.] sexual immorality, theft, false testimony, and slander.” The whole frame of man is#footnote[#emph[Cum primum nascimur in omni continuo pravitate versamur;] we are no sooner born than buried in a bog of wickedness, Tully.] out of frame. The understanding is dark, the will perverse, the memory slippery, the affections crooked, the conscience corrupted, the tongue poisoned, and the heart wholly evil, only evil, and continually evil. Should God chain up Satan, and give him no liberty to tempt or entice the sons of men to vanity and folly, yet they would not, yet they could not, but sin against him, by reason of that cursed nature that is in them, which will always be provoking#footnote[Jude 15, 16.] them to those sins that will provoke and stir up the anger and wrath of God against them. Satan has only a persuading sleight, not an enforcing might. He may tempt us, but without ourselves he cannot conquer us; he#footnote[Jas 1:14.] may entice us, but without ourselves he cannot hurt us. Our hearts have the greatest part in every sin. Satan#footnote[Τὸ πῦρ παρ’ ἡμῶν ἡ δὲ φλὸξ τοῦ πνεύματος. The fire is our wood, though the flame is the devil’s, Nazianzen.] can never ruin a man without the man himself, but a man may easily ruin himself without Satan. Satan can only present the golden cup; he has no power to force us to drink the poison that is in the cup. He can only present to us the glory of the world; he cannot force us to fall down and worship him in order to enjoy the world. He can only spread his snares; he has no power to force us to walk in the midst of his snares. Therefore do the devil so much justice as not to excuse yourselves#super[+] by accusing him, and laying on him the load that you should lay on your own hearts.

#strong[The second #emph[proposition] is this.]

That Satan has a great hand and part in most sins. It was Satan who#footnote[Gen 3:1, 4, 5.] tempted our first parents to rebellion. It was Satan who provoked David to#footnote[1 Chr 21:1.] number the people. It was Satan who put Peter up to rebuking Christ; that is why Christ says, “Get behind Me, Satan!”#footnote[Matt 16:22, 23.] It was Satan who put Cain up to murdering righteous Abel; that is why “he is called a murderer from#footnote[John 8:44.] the beginning.” It was Satan who put treason against Christ into the heart of Judas: “The evening meal was underway, and the devil#footnote[John 13:2.] had already put into the heart of Judas, the son of Simon Iscariot, to betray Jesus.” It was Satan who put Ananias up to lying. Peter said,#footnote[Acts 5:3.] “Ananias, why has Satan filled your heart to lie to the Holy Spirit?” As the hand of Joab was in the story of the woman of Tekoa, so Satan’s hand is usually in all the sins that men commit. Such is Satan’s malice against God, and his envy#footnote[#emph[Diabolus tentat, Deus probat] \[The devil tempts, God tries\]. Tertullian.] against man, that he will have a hand one way or another in all their sins, though he knows that all the sins that he provokes others to will be charged on him, to his greater woe and eternal torment. Ambrose brings in the devil boasting against Christ and claiming Judas as his own: “He is not yours, Lord Jesus; he is mine. His thoughts beat for me. He eats with you, but is fed by me. He takes bread from you, but money from me. He drinks wine with you, and sells your blood to me.” Such is his malice against Christ, and his wrath and rage against man, that he will take every advantage to draw men to what may give him the advantage to triumph over Christ and men’s souls forever.

#strong[The third #emph[proposition] is this.]

That Satan must have a double leave before he can do anything against us. He must have leave from God, and leave from ourselves, before he can act anything against our happiness. He must have his commission from God, as you may see in the example of Job. Though#footnote[Job 1:11, 12; 2:3, 4, 5.] the devil had malice enough to destroy him, yet he did not have so much as the power to touch him till God gave him a commission.

The devils could not so much as enter#footnote[Luke 8:32.] the swine without leave from Christ. Satan would gladly have fought with Peter, but he could not do this without leave: “Satan has asked to sift you#super[+].”#footnote[Luke 22:31.] So Satan could never have overthrown Ahab and Saul but by#footnote[1 Kgs 22.] a commission from God. Ah, what a heart-medicine, what a comfort this should be to the saints, that their greatest, most cunning, and most watchful enemy cannot hurt or harm them without leave from him who is their sweetest Savior, their dearest husband, and their choicest friend!

And as Satan must have leave from#footnote[#emph[Adversaria potestas non habet vim cogendi, sed persuadendi] \[The adverse power has no strength to compel, but only to persuade\]. Isidore.] God, so he must have leave from us. When he tempts, we must assent; when he makes offers, we must listen; when he commands, we must obey; or else all his labor and temptations will be in vain, and the evil that he tempts us to will be put down to his account alone. There is a remarkable passage of the apostle in Acts 5:3: “Why has Satan#footnote[Acts 5:3.] filled your heart to lie to the Holy Spirit?” etc.

He does not reason the matter out with Satan. He does not say, “Satan, #emph[why]#footnote[They are the worst and grossest liars who pretend to religion and the Spirit, and yet are moved only by carnal principles to carnal ends.] have you filled Ananias’ heart to make him lie to the Holy Spirit?” But he reasons the case out with Ananias. Peter said, “Ananias, why has Satan filled your heart to lie to the Holy Spirit?” Why have you given him an advantage to fill your heart with unbelief, hypocrisy, and obstinate audacity, to lie to the Holy Spirit? As if he had said, “Satan could never have done this in you (which will now ruin you forever) unless you had first given him leave.” If, when a temptation comes, a man cries out and says, “Ah, Lord! Here is a temptation that would force me, that would deflower my soul, and I have no strength to withstand it. Oh, help, help, for your honor’s sake, for your Son’s sake, for your promise’s sake!” it is a sign that Satan has not gained your#super[+] consent, but has committed a rape on your souls, which he will pay for dearly.

#strong[The fourth proposition is this.]

That no weapons but spiritual weapons will be useful and serviceable to the soul in fighting and combating with the devil. The apostle shows this: “Therefore take up” (says#footnote[Eph 6:13.] he) “the full armor of God, so that when the day of evil comes, you#super[+] will be able to stand your ground, and having done everything, to stand.” So the same apostle tells you#super[+] that “the weapons of your warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God to#footnote[2 Cor 10:4.] demolish strongholds.” You#super[+] have to deal not with a weak but with a mighty#footnote[We read of many who, out of greatness of spirit, could do violence to nature, but were at a loss when they came to deal with a corruption or a temptation.] enemy. And therefore you#super[+] need to see to it that your weapons are mighty, and that they cannot be unless they are spiritual. Carnal weapons have no might or spirit in them toward making a conquest of Satan. It was not David’s sling or stone that gave him the honor and advantage of setting his feet on Goliath, but his faith in the name of the Lord of Hosts: “You come#footnote[1 Sam 17:45.] against me with sword and spear and javelin, but I come against you in the name of the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts, the God of the armies of Israel, whom you have defied.” He who#footnote[Heraclius’s #emph[motto] was #emph[(a Deo victoria),] it is God who gives victories; and that should be every Christian’s motto.] fights against Satan in the strength of his own resolutions, constitution, or upbringing will certainly flee and fall before him. Satan will be too hard for such a soul, and lead him captive at his pleasure. The only way to stand, conquer, and triumph is always to plead, “It is written,” as Christ did. There is no sword but the two-edged sword of the#footnote[Matt 4:10.] Spirit that will be found to be proven metal when a soul comes to engage Satan. Therefore, when you#super[+] are tempted to uncleanness, plead, “#emph[It is] written, ‘Be holy, because I am holy.’ And#footnote[1 Pet 1:15, 16. 2 Cor 7:1.] ‘let us cleanse ourselves from everything that defiles body and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God.’” If he tempts you#super[+] to distrust God’s providence and fatherly care of you, plead, “It is written, ‘Those#footnote[Ps 34:9.] who fear the #smallcaps[Lord] lack no good thing.’ It is written, ‘The #smallcaps[Lord] gives#footnote[Ps 84:11.] grace and glory; He withholds no good thing from those who walk with integrity.’” If he tempts you#super[+] to fear that you will faint and fall, and never be able to run to the end of the race that is set before#footnote[Job 17:9.] you, plead, “It is written, ‘The righteous one holds to his way, and the one with clean hands grows stronger.’ It is written, ‘I will make an everlasting covenant with them: I will never turn away#footnote[Jer 32:40.] from doing good to them, and I will put My fear in their hearts, so that they will never turn away from Me.’ It is written, ‘Those who wait upon the #smallcaps[Lord] will renew their#footnote[Isa 40:31.] strength; they will mount up with wings like eagles; they will run and not grow weary, they will walk and not faint.’” If Satan tempts you#super[+] to think that, because your sun is for the present set in a cloud, it will therefore rise no more, that the face of God will shine on you no more, that your best days are now at an end, and that you must spend all your time in sorrow and sighing, plead, “It is written, ‘He will again#footnote[Mic 7:19.] have compassion on us; … You will cast out all our sins into the depths of the sea.’ It is written, ‘For a brief moment I forsook#footnote[Isa 54:7.] you, but with great compassion I will bring you back.#footnote[Verse 8.] In a surge of anger I hid My face from you for a moment, but with everlasting kindness I will have compassion on you,’ says the #smallcaps[Lord] your Redeemer. It is written, ‘Though the mountains may be#footnote[Verse 10.] removed and the hills may be shaken, My loving devotion will not depart from you, and My covenant of peace will not be broken,’ says the #smallcaps[Lord], who has compassion on you. It is written, ‘Can a woman#footnote[Isa 49:15.] forget her nursing child, or lack compassion for the son of her womb? Even if she could forget, I will not forget you! Behold, I have inscribed you on the#footnote[Verse 16.] palms of My hands; your walls are ever before Me.’” If ever you#super[+] would be too hard for Satan, and after all the assaults on you have your bow remain in strength, then take to you the word of God, “which is the two-edged sword of the Spirit,”#footnote[Eph 6:16.] and “the shield of faith, with which you will be able to quench all the fiery darts of the devil.” It is not spitting at Satan’s name, nor crossing yourselves#super[+], nor leaning on your own resolutions, that will get you the victory.

Luther reports of Staupitz, a German minister, that he acknowledged that before he came to understand rightly the free and powerful grace of God, he vowed and resolved a hundred times against some particular sin, and could never get power over it. At last he saw the reason to be his trusting in his own resolution. Therefore be skillful in the word of righteousness, and in the acting of faith on Christ and his victory, and on that crown of glory that is set before you#super[+], and Satan will certainly “flee from you,”#footnote[Jas 4:7.] etc.

#strong[The fifth #emph[proposition] is this.]

That we may read much of Satan’s nature and disposition in the various names and titles that are given him in the Scriptures. Sometimes he is called #emph[Behemoth,] that is, #emph[bruta] \[brute beasts\], by which#footnote[Job 40:15.] the greatness and brutishness of the devil is pictured. Those evil spirits are sometimes called διάβολοι, accusers, for their slanders and false charges; and πονηροί, evil ones, for their malice. Satan is #emph[Adversarius,] an adversary who troubles#footnote[Rev 9.] and molests; #emph[Abaddon] is a destroyer. They are called #emph[tempters] for their suggestions, #emph[lions] for their devouring, #emph[dragons] for their cruelty, and #emph[serpents] for their cunning, etc. As his names are, so is he. As face answers to face, so Satan’s names answer to his nature. He has the worst names and the worst nature of all created creatures, etc.

#strong[The sixth and last #emph[proposition] is this.]

That God will soon tread#footnote[Rom 16:20, συντρίψει, #emph[suntripsei,] from #emph[suntribo:] the Greek word means to break or crush a thing to pieces. Applied to the feet, it signifies the breaking or crushing that is done by stamping on a thing.] Satan down under the saints’ feet. Christ our champion has already won the field, and will soon set our feet on the necks of our spiritual enemies. Satan is a defeated adversary; Christ has led him captive and triumphed over him on the cross. Christ has already overcome him, and has put weapons into your#super[+] hands, so that you may overcome him too and set your feet on his neck. Though Satan is a roaring lion, yet Christ, who is “the Lion of the tribe of Judah,” will make Satan flee and fall before you#super[+]. Let Satan do his worst, yet you#super[+] will have the honor and the happiness of triumphing over him. Cheer up, you#super[+] precious sons of Zion, for the certainty and sweetness of victory will abundantly repay you for all the pains you have taken in resisting Satan’s temptations. The broken horns of Satan will be trumpets of our triumph and the cornets of our joy, etc.
